# inc writes x, which it does not declare, and nothing establishes x in inc: it has no formal x, the unit has
# no x in its sight (first_x's is a local of first_x, not visible outward -- Q51), and inc reads x only in the
# tail of `x: x + 1` itself.  By the author's decision of 2026-10-05 (LMX_blog/2026-10-05.md#unknown-head-return;
# HEAD-ROLE-UNESTABLISHED-ROWS) that statement defines a named Structure x; the read in its own tail establishes
# nothing.  So inc has no input x, and neither call of inc needs one: not the root's, not with_caller's, whose
# formal x is no input of inc.  The body of x is inert -- nothing calls x -- and the x it reads is a free name of
# that body, which a caller of x could give (#construction, #dynamic): nothing is missing.  inc returns 7 to both
# callers, and the root sees 7 and 7.  Entry 7.
# Until 2026-10-05 this row expected x to be inc's hidden input (bafca4c, the author 2026-09-28) and the root's
# call inc() refused, "unbound dynamic input x": the reading the author rejected.  Before bafca4c the assignment
# fell back to making x a field of inc, and the program ran.
fn: first_x () int
    int: x
    x: 20
    return: 0
end: first_x
fn: inc () int
    x: x + 1
    return: 7
end: inc
fn: with_caller (int: x) int
    return: inc()
end: with_caller
first_x()
int: a inc()
int: b with_caller(5)
int: ok 80
if: a = 7 && b = 7
    ok: 7
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: ok; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
